---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/url';

// TODO (you): everything below in [square brackets] is a placeholder — replace
// it with your own details and delete the brackets. The section order is
// deliberate: who you are, why the blog exists, what a reader can expect,
// then background. Search engines and first-time readers both want the first
// two answered in the opening sentence.
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About me and this blog — math proofs worked through for fun, and the occasional devlog."
>
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p>
    I'm Abhay — [one line on what you do: e.g. a computer science student at
    X, or a backend engineer at Y]. This blog is where I write up math proofs
    I work through for fun, and occasionally notes on things I'm building.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this blog exists</h2>
  <p>
    [Why you write these up. A genuine reason reads better than a mission
    statement — e.g. "writing a proof out properly is the only way I find out
    whether I actually understood it", or "I kept losing these in notebooks".]
  </p>

  <h2>What you'll find here</h2>
  <p>
    [The topics you expect to cover — e.g. number theory, combinatorics,
    probability puzzles, competitive-programming problems. It's fine to be
    loose; this sets reader expectations, not a contract.]
  </p>

  <h2>Background</h2>
  <p>
    [Short — a few sentences. Education, what you work on, what got you into
    math. This is the part that makes a stranger trust the proofs, so it's
    worth filling in properly.]
  </p>

  <p>
    If you want to reach me, see the <a href={withBase('/contact/')}>contact page</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
